sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-49> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Since purposes can always be reinterpreted as mechanisms, purpose cannot be identified for certain from behavior; the best criterion is that a system is the minimal or optimal one producing a given feature, which suggests it was built for that purpose, as advanced technology should increasingly use minimal systems.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A minimal rule for a feature hints that a system was made for a purpose";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Intelligence in the Universe” (from p. 822), p. 834. Scope: recognizing purpose in artifacts and signals, including extraterrestrial ones"
}
